Vault Integration with CI/CD
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Install and configure HashiCorp Vault on a Linux system
Set up a Jenkins CI/CD server and connect it to Vault using the AppRole authentication method
Build a Jenkins pipeline that retrieves secrets from Vault at build time instead of hardcoding them
Diagnose and resolve common Vault-Jenkins integration failures using a structured troubleshooting script
Apply security best practices for handling API keys and sensitive data in CI/CD workflows
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic Linux command line skills (package management, systemd, file permissions)
Familiarity with CI/CD concepts and pipeline stages
Basic knowledge of JSON/HCL configuration syntax
Understanding of token-based API authentication
Experience with Git
This lab uses a bare Linux machine (Ubuntu 20.04/22.04) with no pre-installed tools. All required software is installed during the lab. The exact install commands are:

sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo apt install -y curl wget unzip git jq software-properties-common apt-transport-https ca-certificates gnupg lsb-release netcat-openbsd
Docker, Vault, and Jenkins are installed in Task 1 with exact commands provided.

Task 1: Install and Initialize Vault, Install Jenkins
Step 1.1: Install HashiCorp Vault
# Create directory for Vault
sudo mkdir -p /opt/vault
cd /opt/vault

# Download and install Vault
VAULT_VERSION="1.15.2"
wget https://releases.hashicorp.com/vault/${VAULT_VERSION}/vault_${VAULT_VERSION}_linux_amd64.zip
sudo unzip -o vault_${VAULT_VERSION}_linux_amd64.zip
sudo mv vault /usr/local/bin/
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/vault
vault version

# Create vault system user and data directories
sudo useradd --system --home /etc/vault.d --shell /bin/false vault || true
sudo mkdir -p /etc/vault.d /opt/vault/data /opt/vault/logs /opt/vault/audit
sudo chown -R vault:vault /etc/vault.d /opt/vault
Create the configuration and systemd service:

sudo tee /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl > /dev/null <<EOF
ui = true
disable_mlock = true

storage "file" {
  path = "/opt/vault/data"
}

listener "tcp" {
  address     = "0.0.0.0:8200"
  tls_disable = 1
}

api_addr = "http://127.0.0.1:8200"
cluster_addr = "https://127.0.0.1:8201"
EOF

sudo chown vault:vault /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl
sudo chmod 640 /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl

sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/vault.service > /dev/null <<EOF
[Unit]
Description=HashiCorp Vault
Requires=network-online.target
After=network-online.target
ConditionFileNotEmpty=/etc/vault.d/vault.hcl

[Service]
Type=notify
User=vault
Group=vault
ProtectSystem=full
ProtectHome=read-only
PrivateTmp=yes
PrivateDevices=yes
SecureBits=keep-caps
AmbientCapabilities=CAP_IPC_LOCK
Capabilities=CAP_IPC_LOCK+ep
CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_SYSLOG CAP_IPC_LOCK
NoNewPrivileges=yes
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/vault server -config=/etc/vault.d/vault.hcl
ExecReload=/bin/kill --signal HUP \$MAINPID
KillMode=process
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5
LimitNOFILE=65536
LimitMEMLOCK=infinity

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable vault
sudo systemctl start vault
sudo systemctl status vault --no-pager
Step 1.2: Initialize, Unseal, and Prepare Secrets
export VAULT_ADDR='http://127.0.0.1:8200'
echo 'export VAULT_ADDR="http://127.0.0.1:8200"' >> ~/.bashrc

cd /opt/vault
vault operator init -key-shares=5 -key-threshold=3 > vault-init.txt
cat vault-init.txt

UNSEAL_KEY_1=$(grep 'Unseal Key 1:' vault-init.txt | awk '{print $4}')
UNSEAL_KEY_2=$(grep 'Unseal Key 2:' vault-init.txt | awk '{print $4}')
UNSEAL_KEY_3=$(grep 'Unseal Key 3:' vault-init.txt | awk '{print $4}')
ROOT_TOKEN=$(grep 'Initial Root Token:' vault-init.txt | awk '{print $4}')

vault operator unseal $UNSEAL_KEY_1
vault operator unseal $UNSEAL_KEY_2
vault operator unseal $UNSEAL_KEY_3

export VAULT_TOKEN=$ROOT_TOKEN
echo "export VAULT_TOKEN=$ROOT_TOKEN" >> ~/.bashrc

vault status
Enable the KV v2 secrets engine and store sample CI/CD secrets:

vault secrets enable -path=secret kv-v2

vault kv put secret/ci-cd/api-keys \
    github_token="ghp_example_github_token_12345" \
    docker_registry_password="docker_password_example" \
    database_password="db_secret_password_123"

vault kv put secret/ci-cd/app-config \
    app_secret_key="super_secret_app_key_456" \
    jwt_secret="jwt_signing_secret_abc"

vault kv get secret/ci-cd/api-keys
Enable audit logging so later troubleshooting has evidence to inspect:

sudo chown vault:vault /opt/vault/audit
vault audit enable file file_path=/opt/vault/audit/vault-audit.log
vault kv get secret/ci-cd/api-keys
sudo tail -n 5 /opt/vault/audit/vault-audit.log
Step 1.3: Install Jenkins
sudo apt install -y openjdk-11-jdk
java -version

curl -fsSL https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable/jenkins.io-2023.key | sudo tee /usr/share/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc > /dev/null
echo deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc] https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable binary/ | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/jenkins.list > /dev/null

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y jenkins

sudo systemctl start jenkins
sudo systemctl enable jenkins
sudo systemctl status jenkins --no-pager

sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword
Complete the setup wizard at http://localhost:8080:

Paste the initial admin password.
Choose Install suggested plugins.
Create an admin user.
Confirm the Jenkins URL as http://localhost:8080/.
Then install the Vault plugin:

Go to Manage Jenkins > Plugins > Available plugins.
Search for HashiCorp Vault Plugin and install it (this pulls in the Credentials Binding dependency automatically).
Restart Jenkins when prompted.
