Expected Outcomes
A running Vault server (unsealed, KV v2 enabled) integrated with a running Jenkins server via AppRole authentication
A Jenkins pipeline that retrieves secrets from Vault at runtime and never exposes them in plaintext in the build log
A reusable troubleshooting script that can independently diagnose Vault connectivity, Jenkins connectivity, policy misconfiguration, and end-to-end secret retrieval failures
Conclusion
In this lab you built a complete Vault-to-Jenkins secrets pipeline from the ground up: installing and unsealing HashiCorp Vault, enabling the KV v2 secrets engine, installing Jenkins and its Vault plugin, and binding the two systems together with AppRole authentication and a least-privilege policy. You then proved the integration worked by running a real Jenkins pipeline that pulled GitHub, database, and JWT secrets out of Vault at build time and confirmed through the console output that only secret lengths were logged, never plaintext values. Finally, you built and exercised a four-stage diagnostic script that checks Vault connectivity, Jenkins connectivity, policy and AppRole bindings, and end-to-end secret retrieval, giving you a repeatable tool for diagnosing this class of integration in any future environment. The key takeaway is that secrets management in CI/CD is not just about storing values securely, it is about designing the authentication boundary (AppRole, scoped policies, short TTLs) so that a compromised pipeline can only ever reach the exact secrets it needs. From here, natural next steps include enabling Vault's database secrets engine for fully dynamic, auto-expiring database credentials, adding TLS to the Vault listener instead of running in tls_disable mode, and wiring the rotation and multi-environment policy patterns from this lab into a real multi-branch Jenkins pipeline.
